{
	title: 'Plugins: owned, hot-reloaded function hooks',
	spec: `Plugins: hot-reloaded files that hook functions on module objects, owned per file.

Files: every \`plugins/*.ts\` in the Hal home (HAL_HOME or the repo root, beside local.ts) is a plugin. Each Hal process (host, and a terminal peer) loads them at startup, after modules are imported and after local.ts, and watches the directory with fs.watch. Web is out of scope. A plugin exports \`default (plugin) => { ... }\`; importing the file must do nothing else operational. Optional \`export const expires = '2026-09-29T16:00:00Z'\`: an absolute UTC ISO timestamp; anything else is a load error.

API on \`plugin\` (the registration function's argument), target = module object + key, typechecked:
- plugin.before(obj, 'key', (...args) => void): observes the arguments; its return value is ignored.
- plugin.after(obj, 'key', (result, args) => void): observes the return value exactly as returned (a Promise for async functions, not its resolved value); ignored return.
- plugin.around(obj, 'key', (fn, ...args) => result): replaces the call; may call fn (bound to obj) zero or more times and return anything.
- plugin.unload(fn): cleanup run when the file's hooks are removed (reload, delete, expiry), and also for a load that fails or is superseded after registering it: the staged cleanups of a load that never activates run at once, so a timer or connection it started does not leak. An unload registered late (say from a timer) after its version was removed or dropped runs at once. A failed replacement leaves the active version and its cleanups untouched.
- plugin.onChange(fn): reconciliation the author supplies, because hooks only affect future calls and the loader cannot know what caches or state depend on them (a theme repaints; nothing is redrawn automatically). fn({ reason, phase }) runs after the whole hook swap is complete: reason is 'load' (first load), 'reload', 'delete' or 'expire'; phase is 'activate' for the incoming version, 'deactivate' for the outgoing one. On reload the outgoing callbacks run first, then the incoming ones, both seeing the new hooks already active (so a theme edited into an unrelated plugin still repaints away its old colours). On delete or expiry the outgoing callbacks see the file's hooks already removed. A failed or superseded load runs none and leaves hooks unchanged. Callbacks must be repeat-safe; one that throws or rejects is reported loudly and the rest still run; nothing is rolled back. Consistency across async state needs subsystem coordination or a restart, not onChange.
Async: any target, sync or async, can be hooked. around keeps the target's return type, enforced by the inferred TypeScript types (a target returning a value needs a sync replacement; one returning a Promise may take an async callback that awaits fn(...args)); there is no runtime check of function kind (a plain function may return a Promise) and no automatic lifting, awaiting or blocking. A sync target cannot wait for async work: a plugin fetches ahead (in an async registration function, or a timer) and its sync hook reads the cached value, or it hooks an async caller (such as auth.pick around the sync auth.pickAccount); the example shows fetch-ahead. before and after are synchronous observers: their return value is ignored and they are not awaited; after receives the literal return value, a Promise for async targets; awaited sequencing is around's job. The registration function may be async; its hooks stay staged until it resolves, and a load superseded meanwhile by a newer edit, deletion or expiry of the same file is discarded, never activated. A target whose key is missing or not a function is a load error naming the file and target. Changing arguments or results is around's job only.

Composition: one wrapper per patched function, holding a chain; when the chain empties the original function is put back exactly. The wrapper keeps \`this\`, lets thrown errors through, and keeps a sync function sync. Files load sorted by filename, hooks keep registration order within a file; editing a file never moves its hooks relative to other files. The first registered around is outermost; befores run in order before the around chain, afters in order after it. A call already underway finishes with the chain it started with.

Reload is atomic for hooks: on change, the file is re-imported cache-busted and its registration function runs against a staging recorder; only if import and registration both succeed are the old hooks (and unload callbacks) removed and the staged ones activated. A failed load reports loudly with the file path (an info/error line in open sessions and stderr on the host) and keeps the last working hooks. Top-level side effects cannot be rolled back; the example says so. A deleted file's hooks are removed and its unload runs. Expiry: at load, an already expired file's registration function is not run; a loaded file's hooks are removed and unload runs when its expiry passes. Expiry never deletes the file.

/plugins (host command, model-usable) lists loaded plugin files with hook targets (e.g. host/auth.pickAccount around), expiry, content hash and last load error.

Repo: plugins/.gitignore ignores everything but itself and example-plugin.ts, so user plugins stay local by default and a fork can delete it to track its own. plugins/example-plugin.ts is tracked, loads cleanly with its hooks commented out (an empty registration function, so /plugins shows no hooks and no error), and documents the API with: a setting override via around on a config function, an observing before, and adding a harmless tool via after(tools, 'all', m => m.set(...)). No security-looking examples: a hook is not a security boundary. ./test typechecks plugins/*.ts like local.ts.

Temporary plugins (agents' convention, stated in the example comments): name the file after the session, e.g. 158-lil-trace-render.ts, and set expires.

Not in this task: plugin slash commands (command metadata lives in common/commands/list.ts, which clients read; needs its own task), a lifecycle event catalogue, web plugins, comparing plugin sets between host and peers (diagnostic only when added).`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Design discussed in session 158-lil with GPT-6 Sol/Astra reviews: before/after observe only; export default registration enables staging and expiry-without-running; eval decision deferred until plugins are tried (old-Hal eval study: /tmp/eval-usage-report.md, ~5% of calls were durable hot patches).',
		"Bun cannot import a file created after it first resolved in a symlinked directory (/tmp on macOS, so every test home): 'Cannot find module', even cache-busted or via require. plugins.load imports realpathSync(path) + '?v=<gen>', which works; the query string does re-run the file (tested with a top-level counter). Each reload leaves the old module version in memory.",
		'Superseding is one generation counter: every load, removal and expiry bumps state.latest[path]; a load checks it after import and after registration and drops itself if overtaken. The watcher skips events whose content hash equals the last one tried, so a failed file is not retried until it changes.',
		'Target names (host/auth.pickAccount) come from scanning require.cache for the module whose exports include the object. The wrapper is rebuilt if someone else (eval, local.ts) replaced it; their function becomes the new original. main.loadPlugins wires report to stderr, slash.output in open sessions and a warning to clients; startup failures happen before sessions open, so they show on stderr and in /plugins.',
		"Theme plugins: colours are OKLCH values (see common/colors.ts), not hex; write one typed plugin.around(colors, 'key', ...) per colour rather than an Object.entries loop with a cast. A theme repaints via onChange calling terminal.redraw(), which must be a safe no-op before the terminal UI is ready and must drop rendered-row caches so every row repaints in the new colours.",
		"terminal.redraw is render.draw(true), which also drops frame.state.rows (a row's cache key holds only its item's own style, not colours painted inside it such as a bash exit status); it is the default no-op until render.init and does nothing while suspended. around's callback type is wrapped in NoInfer, else a returned OKLCH literal widens to number[] and a typed theme around fails.",
		"Bug fix: render.test.ts 'repaints every row in the current colours' failed when run alone inside GNU screen (TERM=screen/STY make ansi.mono true, so the expected red SGR was empty); ./test unsets STY, hiding it. Invariant: tests that assert colour escapes force ansi.mono = () => false (restored after), so they pass under any TERM/STY and in any order."
	],
	needs: ['9', 'wf']
}
